From eaadea3ec62f079a3636268889c8c75fcf12e4da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Moises Santana <191926848+msantana-01@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 12:59:54 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] platform: panel effects for the presented image

Three commits' worth of host-side presentation, none of which reaches the guest
framebuffer. Guest frame dumps at frames 400, 700, 1000, 1100 and 1200 are
byte-identical to a build from before any of it.

Screen Effect (Off, Scanlines, LCD Grid, CRT) draws a multiply mask over the
finished image using the same destination rect as the guest, so it lands on the
guest pixels without any coordinate arithmetic. Effect Strength sets the depth.

Making one setting hold across window sizes took three attempts, and each
failure is the reason the code looks the way it does. A mask on a fixed guest
multiple snapped the gap to whole sub-columns. Rasterising at the window scale
fixed that but exposed the real limit: one screen pixel cannot be antialiased
into anything visible, because the ramp consumes the band and the peak coverage
collapses. Widening the ramp to fill the lattice made it worse, because a ramp
centred on an edge bleeds past it. What works is rasterising at the on-screen
scale, holding the gap to two screen pixels, and covering each band with a
trapezoid that stops at its edge. The grid draws half a gap per side so
neighbouring borders meet.

CRT adds a phosphor glow to the scanlines. It cannot go in the multiply mask: a
halo is brighter than its source and a multiplier can only darken, so it is a
separate texture composited with SDL_BLENDMODE_ADD. The blur is normalised by
its kernel sum to preserve DC gain; normalising by the kernel peak looks better
on an isolated pixel and multiplies every flat area by the square of that sum,
which blows the screen to white.

LCD Response (F4) gives the panel a response time. One exponential step per
guest frame, coefficient derived from elapsed wall time rather than a per-frame
constant, darkening faster than lightening. Off by default, persisted.

Settings persist on release rather than during a drag, and the strength selector
actually saves - it never did before this.
