 Controllers are enabled automatically. The default mapping uses the D-pad or left stick, physical south/east face buttons for Game Boy B/A, and Start/Back for Start/Select. Guide/Home or L3 opens the settings menu; R3 opens the native game panel when the exact-ROM port supplies one. Escape and Android Back also open the settings menu. While a native panel captures input, its D-pad/left-stick and A/B actions do not reach the guest game.
@@ -36,6 +37,10 @@ The settings menu can remap keyboard and controller gameplay actions and shortcu
 
 The Palette selector changes DMG display colors to green, black and white, or amber. It affects the displayed image; diagnostic frame dumps retain the PPU's original colors. CGB games and DMG games running in CGB compatibility mode keep their hardware color palettes, so the selector is disabled in those modes.
 
+Screen Effect draws a panel over the finished image: Off, Scanlines, LCD Grid, or CRT. It is separate from Scale Filter, which chooses how the guest texture is interpolated while being scaled. Effect Strength sets the depth as a percentage. The gap between pixels is sized against the current window scale, holding it to at least two screen pixels so it neither aliases nor disappears at small window sizes, and the effect fades out entirely below 2x where there is no room for a gap. CRT adds a phosphor glow to the scanlines: whatever sits above the bloom threshold is blurred into a halo and added back, so bright areas bleed the way a phosphor does. It is drawn without curvature or vignette, since this is a handheld screen rather than a tube. These effects change the presented image only: diagnostic frame dumps and every differential or oracle comparison are unaffected.
+
+LCD Response (F4) makes the panel lag the light it emits, as a real twisted nematic screen does. Response ms sets how long the trail lasts; darkening runs faster than lightening, which is what leaves a dark smear behind a moving sprite rather than a symmetric blur. It is off by default because the trail costs some readability on a map. It applies to the emitted colour after the palette, once per guest frame, and loading a savestate drops the trail because it belongs to the state that produced it.
+
 ## Saves and savestates
 
 Battery-backed RAM and MBC3 RTC data are loaded and saved by the SDL platform layer. Savestates provide ten slots per game and are checked against the ROM and state-format version before loading.
